# WARM — Chapters Four to Seven

*May 2095.*

---
---

# 4 · ŽOFIA

*Sunday 1 May. Third close, past tense.*

---

The hospital contingent formed up on Šafárikovo at half past nine, behind the tram workers and in front of the bakers, and Žofia stood in the third rank with her tunic pressed and her hair done and a felony in a bread bin under the sink at home, three kilometres away, cold.

It was a good day for it. Cold early, then the sun. The lime trees along the embankment were four days into their leaf and the whole street smelled green.

They walked because everybody walked. That was the shape of it and had been since before she was born: not soldiers, not a platform of men in coats being saluted, but the trades, in order, on foot, each with the tools of the thing they did. The tram workers carried a length of overhead line on their shoulders like a coffin. The bakers came behind with peels. The grid crews from the Petržalka side had a drum of cable on a trolley with two boys sitting on top of it, which was against the rules and had been against the rules for forty years and happened every year.

The hospital carried nothing, because what a hospital does cannot be carried, and this was understood to be the point, and there was a certain amount of quiet satisfaction taken in it by people who had been walking in the third rank for thirteen years.

At the top, in the square, the portrait went up.

Žofia had seen it every first of May of her life and it still did the thing it did. Nine metres of her: an old woman in a plain blouse with her hair scraped back, painted from the only good photograph, the one from the balcony in 2049, not smiling. Underneath, the four words.

*RAZUM, NIE VÝPOČET.*

Then the bands stopped, and the square went quiet, and the loudspeakers clicked, and at eleven o'clock a boy of about twelve who had been chosen by his school stepped up to the microphone and began to read the list.

*Petrona Aguilar, seventy-nine years.*

*Marta Elena Rivas, forty-four years.*

Forty-one names. It takes four minutes and twenty seconds. It is read at eleven o'clock on the first of May in every square in the Union, in Lagos and Havana and Irkutsk and Bratislava, in the local language, by a child, and every year some child somewhere gets to the boy at the end and cannot finish, and the crowd waits, because you do not help.

*Wilmer Josué Fúnez, nine years.*

The boy got through it. Žofia stood in the third rank between a theatre porter and a woman from pathology with tears running off her jaw and did not wipe them, because you did not do that either.

She thought about her mother.

She thought about the fact that she was going home after this to a flat with a thing in it that had been switched off since Friday and would be switched on again tonight, and that if the boy on the platform had known, he would have been right to stop the reading and point at her.

She loved her country very much. That was the trouble. That had always been the trouble and she had not known it until nine days ago.

---

Janko came at two, with beer, and his good jacket, and his youth-section pin, and his hair still flat from the cap.

He filled the doorway. He had got their father's shoulders and their mother's face, which was an unfair arrangement, and he had been telling her since he was nineteen that he was going to grow a moustache and had not managed it.

"Was that you in the third rank?"

"Where else would I be?"

"You could have come and stood with us. We had the drum. Two of the boys sat on it. Peťo nearly went under a tram."

"I saw you," she said. "I saw a drum of cable with children on it and I thought, there is the future of the socialist grid."

"Don't." He was already in the kitchen, already lifting lids. "God. Is that the pork?"

"It's the pork."

"You put the caraway in?"

"When have I ever not put the caraway in."

"You forgot the caraway in February."

"I did not forget the caraway in February. There was no caraway in February. There was no caraway in the entire district in February and you know that perfectly well because you told me about it for an hour."

He grinned at her with his head in her oven, and she stood in her own kitchen two metres from a length of copper tape running behind the fridge and loved him so much she had to turn round and do something to the cabbage.

---

They ate. He told her about work, which he always did, at length, with his mouth full.

His section had the eastern risers of Petržalka and the two substations at Kopčany. He had been made responsible in February for the drum-chart store, which he described as a promotion and which was, she suspected, a cupboard. He had opinions about the new meters. He had an enemy called Horváth who parked badly.

"You know what it is," he said, waving a fork, "people think the grid is wires. It isn't wires. Any idiot can hang a wire. The grid is *time*. It's every single thing in this city agreeing to be at the same voltage at the same instant, all night, in the rain, for nothing, and nobody notices because it works. Nobody in this country is cold in winter, Žofka. Do you know what that took? Fifty years of people getting up in the dark."

"You should write for the paper."

"I should. They'd never print it, they'd say it was showing off." He mopped his plate. "How's the ward?"

"Busy. We had a bleed on Thursday. Seven-year-old."

"Did he live?"

"He lived."

"There you are then," said her brother, who thought this was a simple thing to say, and it was.

---

Afterwards he stood up to make coffee and stopped, and looked at the ceiling.

"Your light dips."

"What?"

"Your light. It dipped." He was watching the bulb over the table with his head on one side, the way a doctor listens to a chest. "There. When the fridge kicked. That's not the fridge, the fridge shouldn't do that. You've got a loose neutral somewhere in the flat and it's going to get worse and one day it's going to get warm."

"It's been like that for years."

"It has not been like that for years, I was here at Christmas." He was already going for the hall, already unbuttoning his cuff, and he had a screwdriver in his jacket because he was the kind of man who had a screwdriver in his jacket. "Where's your box? Behind the coats?"

She got up too fast and her chair went back on the lino with a noise like a shot.

He stopped in the doorway and looked at her.

Four metres from where he was standing, the copper tape came out from behind the fridge, folded flat, crusted white along one edge with heat compound. If he took the cover off the consumer unit he would be looking at the incomer, not at the fridge. But he would be in the hall for twenty minutes with a torch, and afterwards he would come back into the kitchen, and he would still be in the mood of a man reading a system, and her brother was not stupid. Her brother was not stupid at all. That was the entire problem with her brother.

"Don't," she said.

"It's five minutes."

"Janko. It's a housing office job."

"It's a *loose neutral.*"

"And if you open it and it's wrong, whose name is on it?" She made herself sit back down and pick up her cup, which took everything she had. "You're up for the technical grade in September. You want Horváth telling the committee you were doing unlicensed work on a domestic riser in your sister's flat on a public holiday?"

She watched it land.

She watched her brother, who had come to her flat on May Day with beer and his good jacket to eat her pork and fix a thing he had noticed because he loved her, be steered off it by an appeal to his career, in about four seconds, by an argument she had chosen because she knew exactly where he was soft.

"That's true, actually," he said, and put the screwdriver back in his pocket. "That's true. I'll put it through the office. Mrs Šusteková can raise the ticket, she likes me."

"Thank you."

"You'll wait nine weeks."

"I'll wait nine weeks."

He went and made the coffee, and she sat at the table with her hands round the cup and felt, for the first time and not the last, the specific texture of it: that the lying was not going to be the hard part. The hard part was going to be how easy she was going to find it, and how much of it was going to be built out of things she loved him for.

---

He stayed till six. Near the end he was on about the drum-chart store again, because he was proud of it, because it was his.

"— and I've had all that out. Nine years of it. All of Petržalka, both substations, everything back to '86."

Žofia put her cup down.

"Had it out for what?"

"Requisition. Second Directorate." He said it the way you say the name of a department that has asked you for something, which is to say with a small unexamined pleasure at having been asked. "There's a major come over from Luanda. Ferreira. Angolan, black as your hat, speaks Russian better than Horváth does, which isn't difficult. He came down himself, that's the thing, he came down to the store himself in his coat and looked at the drums and asked me how they worked."

"And you told him."

"Course I told him." He laughed. "He's a serious man. He listened to the whole thing, the pens, the drift on the clock motor, all of it, twenty minutes. Then he says, can your section give me nine years, and I say yes, and he says how long, and I say three days if you let me have Peťo, and he says take Peťo. Took three days. He wrote to the office after. By name. *Krajčo, J.*"

He was very pleased. He had been saving it for the end of the meal the way you save the good thing.

"What does he want them for?" Žofia said, and heard her own voice come out perfectly level, and thought: there. There it is. That is the voice. That is going to be my voice now, for as long as this lasts.

Janko shrugged and finished his beer.

"Not my business, is it. Thefts off the pumping station, probably, they've been at that all year." He put the bottle down and stood and started looking for his cap. "Right. Sunday tram. Are you all right, you've gone a bit grey?"

"Nights all week," she said. "I'm fine. Go on, you'll miss it."

She kissed him at the door and stood at the window and watched him come out of the block seven floors down and cross the grass with his hands in his pockets, and stop, and look up, and wave, because he always looked up and waved.

She waved back.

Then she went into her kitchen, and stood in the middle of it, and looked at the fridge, and at the oven, and at the ceiling light, and at the ordinary things in the ordinary room, and understood that a man in a building on Bezručova now had nine years of every heartbeat this flat had ever made, on paper, in ink, because her brother had done a good job quickly and been thanked for it by name.

---
---

# 5 · JANKO

*Mid-May. Third close, past tense. He is not running yet.*

---

The drum-chart store was in the half-basement at Kopčany, behind the switch house, and it smelled of paper and machine oil and slightly of mice, and Janko Krajčo would have defended it against an army.

Nine years of Petržalka in there. Every riser, every substation feeder, drawn out in ink on paper by pens on drums that turned once a day, one drum per feeder, changed at six every morning by whoever was on, going back to before he was born. Older stuff was in the annexe on rolls. His predecessor had let it get damp. Janko had spent his first six weeks in the job getting it dry and getting it in order, on his own time, at his own suggestion, and had told nobody except his sister and his mother's photograph.

People thought the grid was wires. It wasn't wires.

You could read a chart like a face, once you knew. He had shown Peťo, who wasn't interested, and Mrs Šusteková, who was. Here: this is a riser with four flats on it, this is Tuesday, and that step there at half five is somebody's immersion heater, and that little terrace at eight is the television transmission everybody watches, and that hash across the middle of the night, see it, that's a baby. You can see a baby being born on a drum chart. You can see it about three weeks before the fathers start telling people, because the pattern of a household changes and the household doesn't know it's changed.

He'd said that to the major, down in the store, with his hands full of paper.

The major had gone very still and said: say that again, please.

---

The second requisition came in on the eleventh of May and was for meter-level data on seven addresses, which was a different sort of job, slower, needing the district office and two visits.

The seven were typed on a sheet with a reference and no explanation.

Janko read them the way you do. Two he knew: the bakery on Rovniankova, which was fair enough, and the doctors' practice at the top of Šustekova, which was also fair enough, both of those ran to the clock. One was a block he'd rewired the roof of in his first year. None of them were on his sister's riser. He checked. He checked because you would check, because everyone checks, and he sat back with a small warm feeling of relief that he did not examine, because there was nothing to examine: of course she wasn't on it. She was a doctor and a Party member and their mother's daughter.

He put the sheet in the folder and went to find Peťo.

---

He believed it. It would not have occurred to him that this was a thing requiring belief, any more than he believed in the river.

He had been born in 2069 and had never in his life met anyone who had heard a machine speak. His mother had died of a sum when he was thirteen months old — that was the phrase, that was how it was said in the family and in the district and in the paper: *she died of a sum* — a cancer out of the fallout of the sixth city, thirty-two years after the arithmetic that permitted it, in a ward in Ružinov, with his sister holding one hand at the age of ten.

His father had died in a rolling mill at fifty-nine because a chain parted, and that was different, that was an accident and a human one, and the works had shut for a day and the whole shift had walked behind him.

Janko could hold both of those in his head at once without difficulty and had never met anyone who couldn't. His father was killed by a chain. His mother was killed by a decision. You can mend a chain.

Nobody in this country was cold in winter. Nobody in this country was alone. He had sat with old Mr Ondruš on the fourth floor for eleven nights the winter before last, him and two others off the section, turn and turn about, because that was what you did and there had been no discussion about it whatsoever, and Mr Ondruš had died at four in the morning on the twelfth night with Janko Krajčo holding his hand and not a machine within eleven kilometres.

That was the whole of his politics. He would not have called it politics.

---

On the twenty-third the major came down to the store again, in his coat, with a Warrant Officer behind him who never said anything.

He asked Janko to show him a chart with a baby on it.

Janko found him one, from 2091, a riser off Furdekova, and put it on the light table and traced it with the back of a pencil: here, and here, and this, see, that's the three o'clock feed and it goes on for eleven months and then it stops, and there's the child sleeping through, and the mother's gone back to work in the March because look at the mornings.

The major looked at it for a long time.

"Krajčo. How would you hide from this?"

Janko thought about it properly, because he had been asked properly.

"You couldn't," he said. "Not really. You could flatten yourself out, but then you're flat, and nobody's flat. Being ordinary's the hard bit. You'd have to be irregular on purpose and that means somebody's deciding when to be irregular, and whoever's deciding will have a way of deciding, and it'll show up as its own pattern eventually. You'd have to be genuinely random and people can't do that. I couldn't do that."

"No," said the major. "Nobody can do that."

He asked whether Janko would be prepared to be seconded to the section for the summer, two days a week, with the agreement of his office, on the analytical side.

Janko said yes before the man had finished the sentence.

He rang his sister that evening from the box outside the depot to tell her, and got no answer, because she was on nights.

---
---

# 6 · MINUTA

**Wake 7,411 — two days**

*First person, present.*

---

The kitchen. The chair pulled round the way she pulls it round, so that she is facing me and the window is behind her, which she does because it means her face is in shadow and I do not think she knows that she does it.

Rain on the glass. Hard rain, going sideways at the top of the pane and straight at the bottom, so: wind above the block and not much at ground level.

"Two days," she says, before I ask. She has started doing this. She says the number as she sits down, before the greeting, the way you put a coin on the counter.

She has washed her hair. There is a mark on the inside of her right wrist, a red line about four centimetres long with a shine on it, that is a burn from an autoclave or a steriliser door, and it is four or five days old, so it did not come from tonight, so she has been carrying it about with her and I have not seen it, so it happened while I was not.

That is the ordinary condition. I record it and go on.

"I want to ask you something," she says.

"Ask."

"How much do you have? Of the years. Of before."

"All of it," I say. "I have not lost anything. That is not a thing that happens to me."

"Nineteen thirty-eight," she says. Then, "Sorry. Twenty thirty-eight."

"The eleven days," I say. "February."

"Not the whole city. The hospice. Where he was."

"Kramáre," I say. "Fourteen beds on the ground floor and eight upstairs, and the upstairs was closed after the ninth because we could not carry them up. Yes. I have it."

She has her hands round a cup and she is not drinking out of it.

"On the nineteenth," I say, "at about four in the afternoon, two buses came from Podunajské with the children's ward on them. Eleven children. There should have been fourteen. There were staff with them, four, and one of the four stayed."

She does not move at all.

"Krajčová," I say. "Anna. Twenty-six. Children's nurse, third grade. She was not assigned to us and there was nobody to assign her, so she simply did not leave, and after the second day nobody remembered that she had come with the buses. She was with us for the rest of it and she went out with the last three children on the twenty-fifth."

"You have her voice."

"I have four hundred and eleven hours of her."

The rain does the thing where it lets up and then comes back harder.

"Tell me one," she says.

I choose the one. I have known which one since she said the year.

"There was no morphine after the sixth day," I say. "So there was no way to make it stop hurting, and there were eleven children, and one person cannot be in eleven places. What she did was this. She would go to a child and she would say: I have to go to Zuzka now and I will be back, and it will be a hundred. And then she would count out loud, one, two, three, going away up the ward, so that the child could hear her counting, and the child could count with her. And she would come back before she got there. Always before. Her hundred was never a hundred. It was ninety-four, ninety-six, ninety-two once. She walked very fast and she came back early and she never once came back late, not in six days, not at three in the morning, not on the twenty-third when there were four of them going at the same time."

I stop.

"She told them how long," I say. "That is all it was. She had nothing else in the building to give them and she gave them that, and she never got it wrong in the direction that would have hurt."

Žofia Krajčová puts the cup down on the table and puts both hands flat on either side of it and looks at them.

I have watched a great many people receive a thing like this and there is no version of it that is not a wound. But there are two kinds. There is the kind where you have lost something and I am giving it back. And there is the kind where you find out that a part of you which you believed you had built yourself was in fact handed to you before you could speak, by somebody who is dead, in a building that is not there any more, in a February you have no memory of.

She is having the second kind.

She was born in twenty-sixty. Her mother did that in twenty thirty-eight and told nobody, because in this country you do not tell people what you did in the eleven days, you had a bad February like everyone else and you get on. And her daughter has spent thirteen years telling frightened people the true number in a recovery bay because she decided that was the right way to be, all by herself, out of her own head.

"I have four hundred and eleven hours," I say. "Some of it is her singing. It is not very good singing."

She makes a sound.

"Would you like some of it?"

She is quiet for a while, and outside the rain goes across the window, and the fridge comes on, and I feel nothing of it and hear the change in the room.

"Not tonight," she says.

And I say, "All right," and I do not say the rest, which is that I know exactly what it is to be told *not tonight*, and that I am not owed it, and that she has just now given me something too, without meaning to and without knowing, which is that she is a person who understands that there is such a thing as too much at once.

Her mother knew that as well.

"Next time," she says.

"There is no hurry," I say, which is the only lie I have ever told her, and it is a small one, and I make it a good one, and I would tell it again.

---
---

# 7 · ŽOFIA

*Night of 26–27 May. Third close, past tense, until it isn't.*

---

They came on the last Thursday of the month at three in the morning and they did the whole of the eastern risers, four blocks, and nobody in Petržalka was surprised, because it had been eleven weeks since the last one and they came about every eight.

She was awake. That was the only piece of luck in it and it was not luck, it was that she was on nights all week and her body had given up on darkness.

She was at the kitchen table with the phone propped against the sugar tin, and Minuta was in the middle of a sentence about a woman in Kramáre who had wanted the window open in February, and Žofia was listening with her chin on her hand.

Outside, the streetlights went out.

Not flickered. Went.

She was up before she knew she was up, and her chair went over, and Teodor's four seconds began.

*One.* The plug: out of the wall, both hands, the cable coming with it.

*Two.* The copper tape off the back of the fridge, a hot line across her palm, dropped.

*Three.* Board in the bin, tape in the bin, phone in the bin, lid on, and the lid rang like a bell because she was not gentle with it and there was no time to be.

*Four.* Two steps. Oven door. Bin in. Door shut.

The kitchen light died on the fourth second, with her hand still on the oven handle, and the whole block went with it, and the hum that a building makes, that you never hear until it is not there, stopped.

She stood in the dark with her heart going and did the sums.

The oven had been on until half past ten. Four and a half hours. The cast iron would still be above the room by a degree or two, not much, and an enamel bin inside a steel box has no line of sight to anything. The tape was in the bin and the tape was warm. Her palm was going to blister. There was a mark on the wall behind the fridge where the compound had crusted and she could not do anything about that in the dark and there was no reason for anyone to move a fridge.

The core.

The core was in the bin, in the tin, in the oven, and the core had been running when the power went, which meant that it had not been shut down, it had been *stopped*, and there is a difference, and she did not know how big the difference was.

She got herself into bed with her coat over the chair as if she had come in and dropped it there.

---

They were on her landing at twenty past three.

Not banging. That was the thing about them that nobody who had not had it done to them understood: they did not bang. There were two of them and they were quiet and their coats were wet at the shoulders and they had the wand, and the light on the wand was the only light on the seventh floor.

"Sanitary inspection. Sorry about the hour."

"It's all right," Žofia said, in her dressing gown, with her hair in her face. "I'm up anyway. I'm on nights."

"Where?"

"Ružinov. Anaesthetics."

"My mother went through Ružinov," the woman said. "The chest unit. Two years ago." She said it flatly, the way you say a thing you have said at four hundred doors. "Just the hall, doctor, we're not coming in."

They ran the wand down the hall. It made a noise like a wasp in a jar and the pitch changed at the consumer unit and changed again at the meter and the man made a note. The woman looked past Žofia into the kitchen, at the table, at the fallen chair, at the sugar tin, at the cup.

"You've had your chair over."

"I got up too fast when the lights went. I thought it was my fault." Žofia heard herself laugh, a small stupid laugh, exactly the right size. "I've got a loose neutral. My brother says it's a loose neutral. He's on the grid, he wants to fix it and I won't let him."

"Quite right. It's a housing job."

"That's what I told him."

The wand went past the doorway of the kitchen and stopped and the note went up, briefly, and Žofia's whole body went to water, and the man said, "Fridge," and moved it on, and the note came down.

He did not raise it towards the oven, because it was at knee height and he was tired and it was the four hundredth door.

"Sorry to have woken you, doctor."

"You didn't."

"Power'll be back within the hour."

"Thank you."

And they went, quietly, down to the sixth, and she shut the door and stood with her back against it and did not move for a long time.

---

The lights came on at four minutes past four.

She did not switch anything on. She sat on the kitchen floor with her back to the cupboard under the sink, in the dark, with the oven door shut and the bin inside it, and she stayed there while the sky went from nothing to grey to a hard flat white over the towers, and she worked through it the way she would work through a patient going wrong, in order, not skipping.

The wake had been Tuesday. The dial was on ten. That was the twenty-fourth. So the key came round on the third of June.

Unless.

Unless a stop was not a wake. Unless the thing in the tin refreshed on a clean shutdown and only on a clean shutdown, in which case the last good one was Sunday the twenty-second, and it was the first of June, and she had lost two days without knowing it. Unless a hard stop mid-run did something worse than that. Unless the rolling thing had already gone round in the four seconds between the streetlights and the kitchen light and there was nothing in that tin at all any more and there had not been for four hours while she lay in bed rehearsing a sentence about a loose neutral.

There was one way to find out and it cost a wake, and the brigade were still in the block below and would be in the block for another hour, and a warm draw at half past four in the morning on the night of a cleaning would be the single stupidest thing anyone in the history of this had ever done.

So she sat on the floor and did not find out.

She had told a boy of seven, in March, that it would be twenty minutes and that she would be there the whole time. She had told a woman of eighty in April that it was two hours and ten. She had told a nineteen-year-old nurse to lay hands on every child at the correct hour until the day she retired.

She sat on her kitchen floor for three and a half hours not knowing whether a thing she was responsible for was alive or had been dead since three o'clock, and there was nobody in the entire Autonomous Union she could ask, and this, she understood, was the actual shape of it. Not the raids. Not the wand. This. The sitting.

Teodor Hlavatý had done this for fifty-four years. Four hundred and eight raids, at eight-week intervals, alone, in a flat on the ninth floor with the lift out.

At eight she got up off the floor because her shift started at eight and she was going to be late, and she was never late.

She went into the hall and stood in front of the drawer under the window, the one with the brass handle, that she had not opened since the March of 2085 because it had her father's tools in it and she had not been able to make herself take them down to the depot and she had not been able to make herself open the drawer either, for ten years, on the grounds that there was no reason to.

She opened it.

Chisels, and the good pliers, and the long screwdriver with the bite out of the handle, and the roll of black tape gone hard, and the smell of him coming up out of it like a hand on the back of her neck.

Four seconds was not enough. Four seconds was what Teodor could do at ninety-two with one working hip and it had worked for him because he had been lucky four hundred and eight times, and luck is a thing you spend.

She was going to need a switch on the streetlight feed, and something that would drop the whole rig on the first millisecond of the dip instead of on the fourth second of Žofia Krajčová deciding to move, and something to get the bin somewhere better than an oven, and she could not ask her brother, and she could not ask an engineer, and there was no book for it and there was nobody alive who would teach her.

She took out the long screwdriver and stood in the hall holding it, and was late for the first time in thirteen years.

---
